From a332e2628c587001e3cfeb985d5c2b4977d39348 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zed-patches <zed-patches@localhost>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 21:20:56 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_sandbox: jail limits and a state root per project

Zeo next proposal §6.54 (ask 78), the Zeo half; the ai-jail half is
sys-apps/ai-jail-3.5.0-r1 (the fork's resource limits and --state-root as
patches on upstream 3.5.0).

- agent_sandbox.limits { memory, cpu_quota, max_tasks, disk_quota }, also
  per project, each replacing the default it names. Defaults are the
  operator's values (8G, 400 %, 4096 processes; no disk quota). 0 turns a
  limit off: an unset value inherits, so zero is how a setting drops one.
  They become ai-jail's --memory, --cpu-quota and --max-tasks; a disk quota
  alone brings --max-tasks 4096, because the watchdog that enforces it
  pauses the jail by freezing ai-jail's systemd scope, which exists only
  for a limit (operator, 2026-11-21).
- A limit the installed ai-jail cannot apply (its --help lists no --memory)
  refuses the start: fail closed, cause limits_unsupported, naming the
  package to install.
- agent_sandbox.state_root (default /var/tmp/zeo-jail): each project gets
  <base>/<folder name>-<FNV-1a of the canonical root>, passed as
  --state-root -- its own home, /tmp and Claude config, and so a login per
  project (operator, 2026-11-21). FNV, not DefaultHasher: the folder must
  survive a Zeo update. Zeo creates its claude/ (0700, parents too) before
  the start, so CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=<dir>/claude claude can log in first. It
  replaces --claude-dir and the claude --rw-map; the nesting check guards
  it as it guarded the shared config dir. An ai-jail without --state-root
  keeps claude_config_dir, as before.
- probe reads --state-root and --memory from --help with --forward-port,
  one generic option parser, cached per binary identity as before.
- Limits and the state root join the settings fingerprint, so changing
  them marks a running jail "on but not applied" (§6.50).

- The kill switch (§6.53): the spawned ai-jail's pid now reaches the
  launch registry (StdioLaunch carries a shared cell the v1 and v2 spawns
  fill; a v2 attempt that falls back to v1 spawns twice, the last wins), and
  a Stop button beside the lock of a running jail sends it SIGTERM, which
  ends everything inside (measured, §6.55). control::stop_jail refuses pid
  0 and 1.
- The disk watchdog (§6.54, the operator's design): with a disk quota,
  watch_disk measures the jail's state folder and the project every 5 s
  (allocated blocks, symlinks never followed); past the quota of growth it
  freezes ai-jail's systemd scope through cgroup.freeze -- found from
  /proc/<pid>/cgroup and accepted only if it is an ai-jail-*.scope, so
  Zeo's own cgroup is never frozen -- and the thread shows "Paused: disk
  quota" with Resume (thaw; the quota counts again from there) and Stop
  (thaw, then SIGTERM: a frozen process acts on a signal only once it
  runs). A soft limit: a burst between two measurements can overshoot.

Not yet: directory pickers and the limit fields on the Agent Sandbox page.

Tests: control (kill switch on a real process, scope found from a pid,
never outside an ai-jail scope, the freeze file), disk_watch (sizes,
usage without following symlinks, quota and resume), limits_tests (8): limit flags and zero; a disk quota alone; field-
wise project override; the defaults; a stable, distinct state folder per
project; refusal without --memory; --state-root and the limit flags in the
command with claude/ created 0700; the legacy path without --state-root.
Red seen with the refusal and the state root disabled. agent_sandbox joins
test.sh's CRATES: 0057's own tests had never run there. Fixtures that
predate the limits turn them off with 0. agent_sandbox, settings_ui,
settings_content, agent_servers: 638/638 on bentoo-lab; with agent_ui,
2287/2287.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
